@@ -0,0 +1,290 @@
+# Setup: Linux host, QEMU VM, Asterinas x86-64 kernel
+
+This setup runs syzkaller against Asterinas by using syzkaller's existing
+`linux/amd64` target. Asterinas exposes a Linux-compatible syscall ABI, so the
+Linux syscall descriptions and executor are reused. The VM image still needs
+the same syzkaller basics as a Linux image: networking, passwordless root SSH,
+and enough userspace to run `syz-executor`.
+
+The helper below builds an Asterinas NixOS image with Dropbear SSH and emits a
+`syz-manager` config for QEMU. It compiles `cargo-osdk` from the chosen
+Asterinas tree with `OSDK_LOCAL_DEV=1` so that the kernel and OSDK dependencies
+come from the same release source. New builds enable real, per-thread PC
+coverage by default (`KCOV=1`); use `--no-cover` for an old, uninstrumented
+image or to build with `KCOV=0`.
+
+Both host-side regression tests and in-VM validation (`tools/check-asterinas-kcov.sh`,
+`syz-manager -mode smoke-test`, and continuous coverage-guided fuzzing with
+growing corpus and coverage) are supported. Do not treat config generation or a
+host-only test pass alone as evidence that coverage works in the guest; always
+run the guest KCOV check and `syz-manager` verification steps below when
+testing a new kernel build.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- The Asterinas `v0.18.1` release source (commit
+  `d924a9635a66c7c3bb43e563eaafa2c61d6ee9d5`). The helper defaults to
+  `VERSION=0.18.1` and checks this Git commit for full builds. Use
+  `--allow-version-mismatch` only for experiments with another source revision.
+- The Asterinas build prerequisites, including Nix, QEMU, OVMF, OpenSSL, and the
+  `VDSO_LIBRARY_DIR` environment required by Asterinas.
+- Rust and Cargo with the toolchain specified by the Asterinas checkout; the
+  helper installs a source-matched OSDK under its output directory.
+- The Asterinas KCOV changes: the `KCOV=1` build switch and trace-PC callbacks,
+  `/dev/kcov` with Linux-compatible PC-only ioctls and a shared writable mmap,
+  and `/proc/kallsyms` with `_stext` and `_etext`. An unmodified v0.18.1 kernel
+  does not provide this interface. Apply the KCOV changes as well as the
+  compatibility fixes below before using the default coverage mode.
+- A syzkaller checkout on the Linux host, and `readelf` (binutils) to verify
+  that the coverage object retains its symbols and debug info.
+- KVM access if the generated config keeps `-enable-kvm`.
+
+## Build the Image
+
+Check out the release and apply the compatibility and KCOV patches shipped with this
+integration:
+
+```bash
+git -C $ASTERINAS switch --detach v0.18.1
+git -C $ASTERINAS apply $SYZKALLER/tools/asterinas-v0.18.1.patch
+git -C $ASTERINAS apply $SYZKALLER/tools/asterinas-v0.18.1-kcov.patch
+```
+
+Build syzkaller first:
+
+```bash
+cd $SYZKALLER
+make
+```
+
+Then build the Asterinas image and manager config:
+
+```bash
+cd $SYZKALLER
+tools/create-asterinas-image.sh \
+	--asterinas $ASTERINAS \
+	--out $SYZKALLER/.workdir \
+	--version 0.18.1 \
+	--ovmf /usr/share/qemu/OVMF.fd
+```
+
+The script writes:
+
+- `$SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas.img`
+- `$SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas.id_rsa`
+- `$SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas-syzkaller.cfg`
+- `$SYZKALLER/.workdir/kernel/vmlinux` (coverage mode only)
+
+The helper pins full builds to the development profile (`RELEASE=0`,
+`RELEASE_LTO=0`) and `$ASTERINAS/target`. OSDK's original unstripped ELF is
+`target/x86_64-unknown-none/debug/asterinas-osdk-bin`, not the boot image or
+OSDK bundle, which may have been stripped or transformed. The helper copies
+that ELF byte-for-byte to `kernel/vmlinux` for syzkaller's Linux symbolization
+and coverage-report backend. Coverage generation fails if the original ELF
+is missing or lacks symbols/debug info; it never silently disables coverage.
+
+The generated Asterinas NixOS image uses a minimal stage-2 init to start
+Dropbear without systemd. It installs the public half of the generated SSH key
+for root, sets a random root password hash so key login is not rejected as a
+locked account, and disables password authentication. OpenSSH tools remain in
+the image for syzkaller's legacy SCP transfer. The helper also builds Asterinas
+with `CONSOLE=ttyS0` so syzkaller can collect kernel output from QEMU's serial
+stream.
+
+Asterinas currently configures the virtio guest network as `eth0` with
+`10.0.2.15/24` and gateway `10.0.2.2`, which matches QEMU user networking and
+the host-forwarded SSH port that syzkaller's QEMU backend creates.
+
+To regenerate only the `syz-manager` config for an image that already
+exists, reuse the output directory and SSH key from the original image build:
+
+```bash
+tools/create-asterinas-image.sh \
+	--asterinas $ASTERINAS \
+	--out $SYZKALLER/.workdir \
+	--image /path/to/asterinas.img \
+	--config-only \
+	--kernel-obj /path/to/matching/kernel-objects
+```
+
+`--config-only` requires the existing private key at
+`$SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas.id_rsa`; it does not prove that the image boots
+or that the version under test is fuzzable. Its generated Build ID ends in
+`-unverified`. A full build uses the source tree's actual `VERSION`, Git
+revision, and dirty state in the Build ID, including when
+`--allow-version-mismatch` is used.
+
+`--kernel-obj` is config-only and must contain an unstripped `vmlinux` from
+**the exact KCOV-instrumented kernel installed in that image**. It is copied
+to the output's `kernel/vmlinux`. Omit the option only when the matching ELF
+is still at the default Cargo path above. Keep the original source checkout
+available at `--asterinas`: both `kernel_src` and `kernel_build_src` point to
+it. The script checks for symbols/debug info but cannot verify that a
+config-only ELF matches the guest image or that it was instrumented.
+
+For an old image without KCOV, explicitly opt out instead:
+
+```bash
+tools/create-asterinas-image.sh \
+	--asterinas $ASTERINAS \
+	--out $SYZKALLER/.workdir \
+	--image /path/to/old-asterinas.img \
+	--config-only --no-cover
+```
+
+`--no-cover` does not require a kernel object and omits all three kernel
+object/source paths from the generated config. For full image builds the
+same flag passes `KCOV=0` instead of the default `KCOV=1`.
+
+## Check the coverage interface
+
+Before building, check that the pinned Rust compiler produces real callbacks:
+
+```bash
+cd $ASTERINAS
+tools/test-kcov-instrumentation.sh
+```
+
+The KCOV patch instruments `aster_core` only and keeps OSTD, including the
+recording callback, uninstrumented. LTO is disabled to preserve that boundary.
+This provides synchronous PC feedback for the syscall implementation; it does
+not measure every kernel crate, comparisons, or interrupt/background work.
+
+For an already booted, trusted test VM, the host-side helper builds the static
+ABI test, copies it over SSH, runs it with a timeout, and removes guest temporary
+files. It requires both exit status zero and the real-PC PASS marker; an absent
+KCOV device (status 77) is a failure, not a successful skip:
+
+```bash
+bash $SYZKALLER/tools/check-asterinas-kcov.sh \
+    "$ASTERINAS" "$SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas.id_rsa" "$SSH_PORT"
+```
+
+This requires `cc`, static libc/pthread libraries, OpenSSH, and GNU `timeout` on
+the host. The optional fourth argument is the SSH host (default `127.0.0.1`).
+It uses a temporary known-hosts file with trust-on-first-use, so use it only
+against your isolated test VM. This validates the KCOV ABI, not syzkaller's
+coverage feedback loop.
+
+Alternatively, build the guest-side ABI test on the host, copy it into the
+running guest, and execute it as root:
+
+```bash
+cc -static -pthread -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror \
+    $ASTERINAS/tools/kcov-smoke.c -o kcov-smoke
+scp -O -P $SSH_PORT -i $SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas.id_rsa \
+    kcov-smoke root@127.0.0.1:/tmp/kcov-smoke
+ssh -p $SSH_PORT -i $SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas.id_rsa \
+    root@127.0.0.1 /tmp/kcov-smoke
+```
+
+Use the forwarded SSH port shown by QEMU for `$SSH_PORT`. Require `PASS` and
+exit status zero. Exit status 77 means KCOV is absent. The test checks nonzero
+PCs within the kernel text range, buffer bounds, mapping restrictions, task
+ownership, fork, exit cleanup, and disabling coverage. Then verify syzkaller's
+own coverage feedback as described below.
+
+## Run syz-manager
+
+First run the smoke test. This boots a VM and runs simple programs to confirm
+that syzkaller can copy and execute binaries inside the Asterinas guest:
+
+```bash
+$SYZKALLER/bin/syz-manager \
+	-config $SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas-syzkaller.cfg \
+	-mode smoke-test
+```
+
+The helper can also run this check immediately after creating the image:
+
+```bash
+tools/create-asterinas-image.sh \
+	--asterinas $ASTERINAS \
+	--out $SYZKALLER/.workdir \
+	--version 0.18.1 \
+	--ovmf /usr/share/qemu/OVMF.fd \
+	--run-smoke-test
+```
+
+After the smoke test passes, start continuous fuzzing:
+
+```bash
+$SYZKALLER/bin/syz-manager \
+	-config $SYZKALLER/.workdir/asterinas-syzkaller.cfg
+```
+
+For a new kernel version, leave the manager running for several minutes and
+check that `exec total` continues to increase in its periodic log lines.
+In the default coverage mode, require nonzero and growing coverage as well
+as increasing executions; `coverage=0` is not a successful coverage bring-up.
+Only `--no-cover` intentionally runs without real coverage. A successful
+smoke test alone does not establish sustained fuzzing or coverage growth. Check
+`$SYZKALLER/.workdir/crashes` for any reports from the run.
+For Asterinas panics, the reporter uses the assertion and source path to keep
+different bugs separate. Inspect `crashes/<id>/log0` as well as `report0` for
+the complete raw VM output.
+With one VM, the generated config disables automatic reproduction so a crash
+does not monopolize the only VM. With two or more VMs, it enables reproduction;
+individual crashes can also be reproduced later using `syz-repro`.
+
+The generated manager config intentionally sets:
+
+- `"target": "linux/amd64"` to reuse Linux syscall descriptions.
+- `"cover": true` by default, with `kernel_obj` pointing to the output's
+  `kernel` directory and `kernel_src`/`kernel_build_src` to the Asterinas tree.
+  `--no-cover` sets `cover` to false; only in this opt-out mode does syzkaller
+  tolerate a missing `/proc/kallsyms` and skip module address parsing.
+- `"experimental": { "remote_cover": false }` because Asterinas implements
+  synchronous per-thread PC coverage, not Linux remote/background coverage.
+  Comparison tracing is unsupported: the existing feature probe detects the
+  failed `KCOV_ENABLE(KCOV_TRACE_CMP)` ioctl and leaves comparisons disabled.
+- `"reproduce": false` for the default single-VM configuration, keeping the
+  fuzzer active after a kernel crash.
+- `"sandbox": "none"` to avoid relying on Linux namespace details during the
+  initial bring-up.
+- QEMU disk snapshots enabled at the VM layer, while syzkaller executor
+  snapshot mode remains disabled.
+
+It also disables several Linux-only or high-assumption syscall families such as
+BPF, KVM pseudo-syscalls, io_uring, keyrings, USB pseudo-syscalls, and
+`syz_mount_image`. Re-enable them only after the corresponding Asterinas
+subsystem is known to be ready for fuzzing. The tested Asterinas v0.18.1 tree
+has local fixes for a `PTRACE_TRACEME` parent-lock deadlock and a
+`set_tid_address` panic; verify that the kernel under test has equivalent fixes
+before leaving these syscalls enabled.
+The initial-namespace socket pseudo-syscalls are disabled because Asterinas
+does not currently expose `/proc/self/ns/net`.
+The config also disables the existing KCOV-specific open description,
+`openat$auto_fuse_conn_congestion_threshold_ops_control`, which opens
+`/sys/kernel/debug/kcov` despite its name. This checkout has no dedicated
+`ioctl$KCOV*` or `syz_open_dev$kcov` descriptions to disable; general `ioctl`
+and device-open syscall families remain available.
+
+The guest stage-2 hook tries to create `/sys/kernel/debug/kcov` as a symlink
+to `/dev/kcov` without mounting debugfs. Some sysfs implementations do not
+allow that directory/symlink. All executor KCOV opens (including feature
+probes) therefore use a common helper that prefers Linux's debugfs path and
+falls back to `/dev/kcov` only on `ENOENT`. Other Linux open errors, such as
+`EACCES`, are not hidden by the fallback.
+
+Host-only regression tests can be run without Nix, Rust, or a VM (a C/C++
+compiler and binutils are required):
+
+```bash
+bash -n tools/create-asterinas-image.sh
+python3 -I tools/create-asterinas-image_test.py
+bash -n tools/check-asterinas-kcov.sh
+python3 -I tools/check-asterinas-kcov_test.py
+```
+
+## Notes
+
+The `v0.18.1` release requires local boot/install and kernel fixes for this
+setup. A successful run against the release commit plus these fixes is not a
+claim that an unmodified release image can fuzz. The generated Build ID records
+the source commit and marks a modified worktree as `-dirty`. Passing
+`--allow-version-mismatch` is useful for validating syzkaller plumbing with
+another checkout; it does not prove v0.18.1 fuzzing.
+
+If QEMU cannot find OVMF, pass the correct firmware path with `--ovmf`. If KVM
+is unavailable, pass `--no-kvm` so the generated config uses TCG.
